\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces MODIS EVI distribution for fire events (red) versus non-fire observations (blue) drawn under date-conditional sampling. Left: violin and box plots showing the interquartile range and median for each class. Right: overlapping kernel density estimates with class medians marked by dashed vertical lines. Fire events cluster at lower EVI values (median~=~0.205), consistent with drier, more flammable vegetation, while non-fire cells extend to higher EVI values indicating well-hydrated live fuel. This separation validates EVI as a meaningful live-fuel moisture proxy and justifies its inclusion in the feature set.}}{8}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:evi_dist}{{2}{8}{MODIS EVI distribution for fire events (red) versus non-fire observations (blue) drawn under date-conditional sampling. Left: violin and box plots showing the interquartile range and median for each class. Right: overlapping kernel density estimates with class medians marked by dashed vertical lines. Fire events cluster at lower EVI values (median~=~0.205), consistent with drier, more flammable vegetation, while non-fire cells extend to higher EVI values indicating well-hydrated live fuel. This separation validates EVI as a meaningful live-fuel moisture proxy and justifies its inclusion in the feature set}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {1.2.4}Land-Cover and Interaction Features}{8}{subsubsection.1.2.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Feature group ablation study on the held-out 2024--2025 test set, using mean-substitution on the fitted XGBoost model. Each row reports model performance when the corresponding feature group is replaced by training-set column means, simulating the effect of that group being absent (no retraining required). $\Delta $AUC and $\Delta $Recall are relative to the full model baseline (row 1); larger negative values indicate higher group importance. \textbf  {Meteorology} (temperature, humidity, wind, VPD) is the single most important group by AUC ($\Delta $AUC $= -0.051$). \textbf  {Elevation} is the most critical group for recall ($\Delta $Recall $= -0.144$), consistent with its role as a primary fire-suppressor in the SHAP analysis. Removing the combined \textbf  {Fuel Stack} (EVI + Elevation) produces the largest joint drop ($\Delta $AUC $= -0.107$), confirming that terrain and live-fuel moisture together form the core fire-discriminating signal once weather is held constant. \textbf  {Land Cover} (11 binary indicators) contributes negligible AUC ($\Delta $AUC $\approx 0$) but is retained as a domain-guided structural control. The \textbf  {VPD$\times $Wind} interaction term contributes only marginally on its own ($\Delta $AUC $= -0.0032$), but its physics motivation (fire-spread potential) justifies retention as a domain-guided structural control. $^\dagger $KBDI (Keetch--Byram Drought Index) was evaluated as a candidate feature during feature selection but excluded prior to final model training: its atmospheric drought signal was found largely redundant with VPD, and consistent daily precipitation inputs across India's heterogeneous monsoon zones introduced data-quality issues. Removing KBDI from early experiments produced no measurable change in held-out AUC.}}{9}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablation}{{2}{9}{Feature group ablation study on the held-out 2024--2025 test set, using mean-substitution on the fitted XGBoost model. Each row reports model performance when the corresponding feature group is replaced by training-set column means, simulating the effect of that group being absent (no retraining required). $\Delta $AUC and $\Delta $Recall are relative to the full model baseline (row 1); larger negative values indicate higher group importance. \textbf {Meteorology} (temperature, humidity, wind, VPD) is the single most important group by AUC ($\Delta $AUC $= -0.051$). \textbf {Elevation} is the most critical group for recall ($\Delta $Recall $= -0.144$), consistent with its role as a primary fire-suppressor in the SHAP analysis. Removing the combined \textbf {Fuel Stack} (EVI + Elevation) produces the largest joint drop ($\Delta $AUC $= -0.107$), confirming that terrain and live-fuel moisture together form the core fire-discriminating signal once weather is held constant. \textbf {Land Cover} (11 binary indicators) contributes negligible AUC ($\Delta $AUC $\approx 0$) but is retained as a domain-guided structural control. The \textbf {VPD$\times $Wind} interaction term contributes only marginally on its own ($\Delta $AUC $= -0.0032$), but its physics motivation (fire-spread potential) justifies retention as a domain-guided structural control. $^\dagger $KBDI (Keetch--Byram Drought Index) was evaluated as a candidate feature during feature selection but excluded prior to final model training: its atmospheric drought signal was found largely redundant with VPD, and consistent daily precipitation inputs across India's heterogeneous monsoon zones introduced data-quality issues. Removing KBDI from early experiments produced no measurable change in held-out AUC}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Pearson correlation matrix for the retained feature set, computed on a 500k stratified sample of the training data. Strong correlations among weather variables (VPD--Temperature: $r=0.61$; VPD--Humidity: $r=-0.66$) confirm that VPD summarises the joint fire-weather signal. Elevation exhibits strong negative correlations with temperature ($r=-0.87$), VPD ($r=-0.34$), and EVI ($r=-0.54$), motivating its independent retention. Land-cover binary indicators are orthogonal to weather and terrain features, providing fuel-type information not captured by other features.}}{10}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:corr_matrix}{{3}{10}{Pearson correlation matrix for the retained feature set, computed on a 500k stratified sample of the training data. Strong correlations among weather variables (VPD--Temperature: $r=0.61$; VPD--Humidity: $r=-0.66$) confirm that VPD summarises the joint fire-weather signal. Elevation exhibits strong negative correlations with temperature ($r=-0.87$), VPD ($r=-0.34$), and EVI ($r=-0.54$), motivating its independent retention. Land-cover binary indicators are orthogonal to weather and terrain features, providing fuel-type information not captured by other features}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {1.3}Date-Conditional Negative Sampling Strategy}{10}{subsection.1.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:dcns}{{1.3}{10}{Date-Conditional Negative Sampling Strategy}{subsection.1.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Formal comparison of three negative sampling strategies for wildfire classification. $\text  {FIRMS}(i,j,t)$ is the fire-detection indicator at grid cell $(i,j)$ on date $t$. Only Date-Conditional (DC) sampling eliminates the temporal prior shift that allows classifiers to exploit seasonal shortcuts.}}{11}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sampling_strategies}{{3}{11}{Formal comparison of three negative sampling strategies for wildfire classification. $\text {FIRMS}(i,j,t)$ is the fire-detection indicator at grid cell $(i,j)$ on date $t$. Only Date-Conditional (DC) sampling eliminates the temporal prior shift that allows classifiers to exploit seasonal shortcuts}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Temporal Leakage Theorem (informal).}{11}{section*.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Schematic of the date-conditional negative sampling strategy. For each fire detection (positive sample, red grid cell marked ``F'') on date~$t$, negative examples (non-fire cells on the same date~$t$) are sampled from the same ERA5 meteorological snapshot. The positive sample captures feature conditions at the fire cell; the negative samples capture conditions at non-fire cells under identical regional weather. This design forces the classifier to learn local fire-discriminating signals (EVI, elevation, land cover) rather than exploiting seasonal or weather-regime shortcuts across different dates.}}{12}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sampling_diag}{{4}{12}{Schematic of the date-conditional negative sampling strategy. For each fire detection (positive sample, red grid cell marked ``F'') on date~$t$, negative examples (non-fire cells on the same date~$t$) are sampled from the same ERA5 meteorological snapshot. The positive sample captures feature conditions at the fire cell; the negative samples capture conditions at non-fire cells under identical regional weather. This design forces the classifier to learn local fire-discriminating signals (EVI, elevation, land cover) rather than exploiting seasonal or weather-regime shortcuts across different dates}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {1.3.1}Empirical Validation: Date-Conditional vs.\ Random-Uniform Sampling}{12}{subsubsection.1.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:sampling_experiment}{{1.3.1}{12}{Empirical Validation: Date-Conditional vs.\ Random-Uniform Sampling}{subsubsection.1.3.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Controlled comparison of Date-Conditional (DC) vs.\ Random-Uniform (RU) negative sampling strategies on the held-out 2024--2025 test set. Both models use XGBoost with identical hyperparameters; only the negative sampling strategy differs. DC achieves significantly higher recall (+3.1 pp), confirming it recovers more true fire events. The smaller absolute value of the month--probability correlation for DC ($|r|=0.326$ vs.\ $0.340$) confirms that the DC model is less dependent on the seasonal prior, i.e.\ it exploits the fire-season calendar shortcut less.}}{13}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sampling_experiment}{{4}{13}{Controlled comparison of Date-Conditional (DC) vs.\ Random-Uniform (RU) negative sampling strategies on the held-out 2024--2025 test set. Both models use XGBoost with identical hyperparameters; only the negative sampling strategy differs. DC achieves significantly higher recall (+3.1 pp), confirming it recovers more true fire events. The smaller absolute value of the month--probability correlation for DC ($|r|=0.326$ vs.\ $0.340$) confirms that the DC model is less dependent on the seasonal prior, i.e.\ it exploits the fire-season calendar shortcut less}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Overall performance comparison between Date-Conditional (DC) and Random-Uniform (RU) negative sampling on the 2024--2025 test set. DC achieves higher recall (+3.1 pp) and F1 at a marginally lower precision, reflecting its more aggressive fire-event detection. The header reports the month--probability Pearson correlation for each model; the higher absolute value for RU ($|r|=0.340$) confirms stronger seasonal shortcut dependency. AUC values are similar (0.961 vs.\ 0.960), confirming that the benefit of DC is concentrated in the operational recall metric rather than ranking alone.}}{13}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sampling_metrics}{{5}{13}{Overall performance comparison between Date-Conditional (DC) and Random-Uniform (RU) negative sampling on the 2024--2025 test set. DC achieves higher recall (+3.1 pp) and F1 at a marginally lower precision, reflecting its more aggressive fire-event detection. The header reports the month--probability Pearson correlation for each model; the higher absolute value for RU ($|r|=0.340$) confirms stronger seasonal shortcut dependency. AUC values are similar (0.961 vs.\ 0.960), confirming that the benefit of DC is concentrated in the operational recall metric rather than ranking alone}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Per-month recall on the 2024--2025 test set for Date-Conditional (DC) and Random-Uniform (RU) sampling. The shaded band marks the peak fire season (March--June), where both models perform similarly. The critical divergence occurs during monsoon months (July--September): the RU model's recall collapses to 18\% in July (vs.\ 34\% for DC) and 29\% in August (vs.\ 43\% for DC). This collapse is the hallmark of seasonal shortcut learning --- the RU model has learned that ``July is not fire season'' rather than discriminating fire-prone from fire-safe cells on any given day. The DC model, by construction forced to learn within-date spatial signals, maintains a substantially more stable recall across all months.}}{14}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sampling_monthly}{{6}{14}{Per-month recall on the 2024--2025 test set for Date-Conditional (DC) and Random-Uniform (RU) sampling. The shaded band marks the peak fire season (March--June), where both models perform similarly. The critical divergence occurs during monsoon months (July--September): the RU model's recall collapses to 18\% in July (vs.\ 34\% for DC) and 29\% in August (vs.\ 43\% for DC). This collapse is the hallmark of seasonal shortcut learning --- the RU model has learned that ``July is not fire season'' rather than discriminating fire-prone from fire-safe cells on any given day. The DC model, by construction forced to learn within-date spatial signals, maintains a substantially more stable recall across all months}{figure.6}{}}
